File Index

/security-headers-common.conf
Purpose: The three non-CSP baseline headers (nosniff, Referrer-Policy, X-Frame-Options) shared by every browser-facing block, split out 2026-07-31 so a host can take a different CSP without duplicating them. Sets NO Content-Security-Policy on purpose: nginx APPENDS add_header rather than replacing it, so two CSP headers make the browser enforce their INTERSECTION and a "relaxed" profile stacked on the strict one relaxes nothing. Never included directly by a server block — include exactly one profile (security-headers.conf / -scripting.conf / -api.conf) and it pulls this in.

/security-headers-api.conf
Purpose: The same baseline for API-ONLY hosts (JSON/websockets, never a rendered document), with CSP default-src 'none' — with no document to protect, the strictest policy costs nothing and still matters if a JSON endpoint is ever coaxed into rendering as HTML. Also used to restore headers inside locations that declare their own add_header (see the trap note in security-headers.conf).

/security-headers.conf
Purpose: The STRICT CSP profile — the default for BROWSER-FACING server blocks; include once per server{} (it pulls in security-headers-common.conf for the non-CSP headers). script-src 'self' is verified safe for the apps ON THIS PROFILE — none contains an inline <script> or calls the Function constructor, and Vite emits external module scripts — which is what makes an injected script inert, so keep 'unsafe-inline'/'unsafe-eval' out of it; a host that genuinely needs eval takes security-headers-scripting.conf instead, which keeps the exception scoped to one named host rather than loosening this for everyone. style-src DOES need 'unsafe-inline' because React renders style= attributes, which CSP counts as inline styles; that is style injection, not script execution, so it does not weaken the above. connect-src names the auth hub explicitly because every app (including the .com ones) talks to it cross-origin for device credentials and SSO, plus wss://$host to cover the real-time apps without listing each. THE TRAP: nginx add_header does NOT merge across levels — any location declaring its own add_header silently discards every inherited one, so such locations (/data/, /healthz) must include this file AGAIN. Grep for add_header inside locations before assuming coverage.

/security-headers-scripting.conf
Purpose: The RELAXED CSP profile — same as the strict one but with script-src 'unsafe-eval' and worker-src 'self' blob:. Used by exactly one host, game-client.shatteredarchive.dev, which is the only app in the repo that calls the Function constructor or bundles an interpreter. Added 2026-07-31 after the strict rollout hard-broke that app: fengari-web (Lua) evaluates Function("return ()=>void 0;") at MODULE SCOPE, unguarded, and luaRuntime.ts imports it statically — so the EvalError fired before React mounted and the whole client failed to boot, not just Lua. skulpt (Python) and the user-script sandbox itself (userScripts/runtime.ts, globalRuntime.ts) need it too, so no bundler/library swap removes the requirement — running user-authored code IS the feature. Include INSTEAD of security-headers.conf, never alongside it (see the intersection trap in security-headers-common.conf). worker-src covers the optional @huggingface/transformers STT engine's blob: WASM worker; note its model download from huggingface.co is still blocked by connect-src, deliberately unresolved. 2026-07-31: connect-src gained https://shatteredarchive.com — the C# site service the client calls for cloud-sync and /contribute/*, which was missing, so those fetches died as a bare "Failed to fetch" (a CSP refusal carries no status code and never reaches the server log). No longer included directly: the game-client block selects this file or the -dev variant below via ${NGINX_CSP_SCRIPTING_FILE}.

/security-headers-scripting-dev.conf
Purpose: Dev-machine variant of security-headers-scripting.conf, selected by ${NGINX_CSP_SCRIPTING_FILE} (same idiom as ${NGINX_TLS_INCLUDE_FILE}). Differs on exactly ONE token and must stay that way: connect-src names http://localhost:5000 — where the local stack publishes the C# site service — instead of production's https://shatteredarchive.com. That origin must never reach the production profile, where it would name whatever the VISITOR happens to run on their own :5000.

/tls-dev.conf
Purpose: Configures HTTPS routing and proxying for multiple project services including game client, web client, and API servers. MUST mirror edge-subdomains.conf's HTTP (port 80) blocks route-for-route, one file per scheme — there is no shared/DRY'd routing between the two. Phase F: added kingdom-tactics.shatteredarchive.dev's HTTPS block; a real deploy gap (this block didn't exist for the first deploy pass — https:// 404'd at the default catch-all since kt-server's own KT_CLIENT_ALLOWED_RETURN_ORIGINS is https-only) caught by live-testing the real public hostname end to end, not by review. Any FUTURE new subdomain added to edge-subdomains.conf needs the identical block added HERE too, or it silently only half-works (HTTP fine, HTTPS 404). Scrum Poker (2026-07-28) followed that rule from the start — its HTTPS block landed alongside the HTTP one, and route parity between the two was checked mechanically (both expose exactly /api/scrum/, /ws/scrum, = /health, /). The websocket block matters especially here: the client picks wss:// whenever the page is https, so an HTTPS block missing /ws/scrum would leave a room that loads but never connects. 2026-07-29: the auth + builder edge rate limits were mirrored here the same way — auth gained the new /api/auth/ block and a limit_req on /api/, build gained one on /api/ — because the limit_req_zone declarations live in edge-subdomains.conf (http context, above this include) but the limit_req directives that USE them must be repeated per location in both schemes. Omitting them here would rate-limit HTTP while leaving HTTPS — the scheme everyone actually uses — wide open. The build /api/ client_max_body_size 3m fix (1m built-in default vs the app's 2mb import/snippets routes) is mirrored here for exactly the same reason: HTTPS is the scheme real imports come in over, so fixing only the HTTP block would have left the 413 in place where it actually bites. 2026-07-30: every server block here also includes security-headers.conf (or -api.conf), mirroring edge-subdomains.conf — CSP on the HTTP blocks alone would protect the scheme nobody uses. 2026-07-31, same trap from the other side: the game-client block's CSP was loosened in edge-subdomains.conf ONLY, so the browser (which uses https on this machine) kept enforcing the old policy and the C# site fetches kept failing — this block now includes security-headers-scripting-dev.conf. Note it hardcodes that filename rather than the ${NGINX_CSP_SCRIPTING_FILE} the :80 block uses: envsubst runs over the default.conf.template mount only, never over files in this directory, so a ${...} here would reach nginx literally. Correct anyway — this file IS the dev half of the tls-dev/tls-off pair, so only the dev CSP profile can belong in it.

/tls-off.conf
Purpose: This file serves as a placeholder configuration indicating no local TLS server blocks are included.
